Suppose I have an algebraic surface $S$ over some metric field $K$ with two different elliptic fibrations. I can put a partial orderings $<_1$ and $<_2$ on the fibers of $S(K)$ as follows: we say that $Q <_1 P$ when the multiples of $P$ along the first fibration lie dense around $Q$, and we define $<_2$ likewise for the second fibration. We define a partial order $<$ on the entirety of $S(K)$ by declaring $Q < P$ when either $Q <_1 P$ or $Q <_2 P$ holds, and taking the transitive closure. [Just as a motivating side remark: the idea behind this relation is constructing rational points $Q$ when a single rational point $P$ is given, when $S$ is assumed to be defined over the rational numbers.] Now my question is this: if we restrict the order $<$ to the fibres, do we get back the fibre-wise orders $<_1$ and $<_2$? [In terms of constructing rational points, this would mean: can we construct rational points $Q'$ starting from $P$, lying arbitrarily close to a point $Q$ on the same fibre as $P$, without having $Q <_1 P$ or $Q <_2 P$?]

